From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 17:29:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Makefile.master: look build tools up on `$PATH`

Cross-building illumos from a non-illumos host (here, Nixpkgs on Linux)
means none of the tools the build shells out to live at the absolute paths
`Makefile.master` hardcodes: there is no `/usr/bin/nawk`, no
`$(ONBLD_TOOLS)/bin/$(MACH)/ctfconvert`, and no `/usr/gcc/10`. Every one of
them is on `$PATH` instead, put there by the package manager.

So:

- Reduce the hardcoded `/usr/bin/...` and `$(ONBLD_TOOLS)/...` tool paths to
  bare command names, letting `$PATH` resolve them.
- Take the primary C and C++ compilers, and the linker, from the `$CC`,
  `$CXX`, `$LD` and `$LD_FOR_BUILD` environment variables. These are
  environment variables, not make macros -- the `:sh` assignments run a
  shell, which is where they are read. `|| :` keeps a missing compiler from
  failing the whole build, since not every component needs every compiler.
- `BUILD_CPP`/`NATIVEBUILD_CPP`: use the C compiler's own `-E` rather than
  `/usr/lib/cpp`, which does not exist off illumos.
- `ROOT=/`: Nix builds each component into its own prefix and needs no proto
  area.
- Drop `-errwarn=%all`: building against a different compiler and different
  libc headers than illumos itself uses turns up warnings the in-tree build
  never sees.
- Disable `PROCESS_COMMENT` and `STRIP`, which need `mcs` and an
  illumos-aware `strip`.

This is deliberately blunt -- it changes the defaults rather than making
them overridable -- and so is not upstreamable as-is. Making each tool
overridable via the `$(POUND_SIGN)` idiom already used elsewhere in this
file would be the upstreamable form.

The environment variables read here are `$ILLUMOS_CC`/`$ILLUMOS_LD` rather
than the obvious `$CC`/`$LD`, and that is not cosmetic. SunPro make records
which macros it imported from the environment and re-exports them, with
their *new* value, every time the makefile reassigns one
(`cmd/make/lib/mksh/macro.cc:1220`). `Makefile.master` reassigns both `CC`
and `LD` a little further down, so a recursive `$(MAKE)` -- e.g. the bare
`$(MAKE) all.targ` that `uts/i86pc/unix/Makefile:199` uses to reach
`assym.h` -- receives `CC` in its environment as the whole expanded
`cw --tag target ... --` command line. `command -v $CC` then resolves to
`cw`, and `cw` is handed itself as its own primary compiler. Names that are
never make macros are immune to that at any depth of recursion.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (Claude Opus 5)
